    "encrypted": "false",
    "display_name": "Finding Paradise",
    "long_instructions": "In Lesson 11 (Levels 1-4, \"Decision Trees in Code\"), students work through decision-tree-to-code translations like this one. This particular example is a simpler, single-question tree than the fever/cough example from the slides, just one branch point instead of two, which makes it a good warm-up translation before students tackle anything nested.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n- Fix the `decide` function to help someone decide where their paradise might be:\r\n  - If they want people around, they should look for \"A city.\"\r\n  - If they don’t want people around, they should look for \"Somewhere far away.\"\r\n    \r\n---\r\n\r\n**Reflection:** What might your students find challenging about this task, writing a single if/else correctly, or returning the exact string expected?\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n::: details [**See a solution**]\r\n```\r\ndef decide(wants_people_around):\r\n  if wants_people_around:\r\n    return \"A city\"\r\n  else:\r\n    return \"Somewhere far away\"\r\n\r\n\r\ndef main():\r\n  wants_people_around = False\r\n  decision = decide(wants_people_around)\r\n  print(f\"You should look for: {decision}\")\r\n\r\n```\r\n:::\r\n",
    "bubble_choice_description": "Build a decision tree to find someone's paradise.",
    "thumbnail_url": "https://images.code.org/40961d428f7beac5e389b4647c12511a-paradise.jpg",
